specs/hal-engine-tool-budget/spec.md:66:`maxToolRounds` counts rounds, and a call that never returns never reaches the next one, so the round bound says nothing about time; hence a second bound. The default matches `withRetry`'s `firstChunkTimeoutMs` and is three times the 10000 ms a tool's own HTTP client would typically allow a request, so a well-behaved tool fails first, with its own error; this backstop only fires for a tool that has no deadline of its own or ignores it. That is also why the bound is the race and not the signal: few executors read the signal, and one that does not would hold the round open regardless. The abort's reason is a `TimeoutError`, as `AbortSignal.timeout` gives, so a tool can tell its deadline from its turn being abandoned, and so can the registry, which logs a throw after the deadline as a failure and one after an abandoned turn at `info` ([a tool call that fails](../hal-engine-tool-executor-throw/spec.md)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-budget/spec.md:81:The user-facing sentence itself and routing an exhausted turn to a human, which belong to the consumer (for the support agent, re-cinq/Otto#151); failing over to a second provider; a `stopReason` taxonomy, since `tool_budget_exhausted` is the first engine-normalized value and provider strings otherwise pass through; summing usage across rounds, which touches the same loop; an unknown tool name and input the schema rejects, both about one call failing rather than a call not being made; and per-tool or per-turn cost budgets and a whole-turn deadline. For the per-call deadline: a deadline per tool, since one number covers every tool and an executor can bound itself more tightly; retrying a timed-out call, which the model decides within the round budget; rolling back a downstream write an abandoned call started; and abandoning a run when its socket closes, which [abandon-on-close](../hal-engine-abandon-on-close/spec.md) specifies: the run's signal is composed with each call's own and does not replace its deadline.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:8:This package has never been published. It is `hal-engine@0.1.0`, CommonJS, unlicensed, and reachable only through a git specifier that clones the repository and builds from source. This spec describes what has to become true for `npm install @re-cinq/hal-engine` to resolve from the public registry: the module format it ships, the manifest that describes it, the pipeline that releases it from a published GitHub release without a stored token, and the handful of defects a first public release must not carry — an authentication fallback that opens a chat API, a hook that is declared and never called, a factory arm that drops its configuration, and an optional peer that is not optional. It also covers the documentation, because npm renders `README.md` and nothing else.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:88:Nothing from `src/`, `example/`, `smoke/`, `docs/`, `specs/`, `adrs/` or `scripts/` appears, and the publish workflow fails the job if one does. Sizes move with every source change and are not pinned here; the group table is what a reviewer checks. The publish workflow's pack-list check MUST fail on a `*.map` entry for the same reason it fails on a `*.test.*` one.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:94:`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs typecheck, lint, the traceability backlog report, format, test, build, and three spec-consistency checks. It does not measure coverage.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:162:Nothing in this repository has ever installed the package from a tarball. A git specifier builds from source and therefore exercises neither the `exports` map, nor `files`, nor the dependency list, nor the emitted `.d.ts` files.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:199:- `id-token: write` is scoped to the `publish` job rather than declared at workflow level, so the `verify` job - which runs the tests, the build and the packed-tarball smoke test - cannot mint an OIDC token at all. The issue specified a workflow-level pair; narrowing it costs nothing and removes a capability from every step that does not publish.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:245:- The `v0.2.0` tag is pushed after the bootstrap, on the commit it published, and no release object is created for it. A tag with no release still renders at `/releases/tag/v0.2.0`, which is what the changelog links to, and the publish workflow no longer triggers on a tag push - so the link resolves and nothing runs. Creating a release instead would fire `publish.yml`, pass every gate against the tagged commit and fail at the staging step against a version that already exists: a permanently red run in the history, bought for a row on the releases page. This replaces the earlier plan to accept that red run, which predates the release-event trigger. The attestation on `0.2.0` names the bootstrap workflow, which no longer exists in the tree, and `npm audit signatures` still verifies it, because the attestation records the workflow at the commit it ran from.
